\v 7 \add ≈But¦340060\add* there was¦340060 another large eagle¦340061 with large wings¦340066 and¦340067 many feathers¦340069. Oh wow¦340070, that vine¦340071 turned¦340073 its¦340074 roots¦340074 toward this¦340072 eagle, and¦340076 it¦340077 spread out¦340077 its¦340076 branches¦340076 toward the eagle from¦340082 the¦340082 place it had been planted¦340083 so it might get watered¦340080.
\v 8 It had been¦340094 planted¦340094 in good¦340088 soil beside a large body of \add #water¦340091\add* so it would grow branches¦340096 and¦340097 produce fruit¦340098, to¦340099 become a¦340100 magnificent vine¦340100.’
\p
\v 9 \add ≈Ask¦340104\add* \add the people\add*: ‘The master¦340107 Yahweh¦340108 wants to know if it'll¦340109 prosper¦340109. Won't¦340110 it be uprooted and¦340115 stripped¦340119 of¦340117 its¦340117 fruit¦340117 so¦340120 that it¦340120 withers¦340120, and all¦340121 its fresh¦340123 growth¦340124 will dry out? \add ≈You wouldn't have to be very strong or get lots of people¦340130 to help\add* to pull¦340114 it¦340134 out by its roots.
\v 10 \add ≈So¦340137\add* listen¦340137. After it¦340138 had been¦340138 planted¦340138, would¦340139 it¦340139 thrive¦340139? Wouldn't it¦340142 wither up when the¦340143 eastern¦340144 wind¦340143 touches¦340141 it? \add Yes,\add* it would wither right up in¦340142 the¦340149 garden plot where it¦340151 sprouted.’ ”
