\rem /s1 The Temple
\p
\v 48 Then¦352346 the man took¦352346 me¦352346 to¦352347 the¦352349 temple¦352350 porch¦352349 and¦352351 measured¦352351 its doorposts—they were each two and a half metres¦352355 thick. The entryway itself was seven metres¦352358 wide,\f + \fr 40:48 \ft Following the LXX, as the 1.5m (three cubits) in the Hebrew appears likely to be a copying mistake.\f* and¦352360 the¦352360 walls on each side of¦352360 it were¦352362 one and¦352365 a half metres¦352366 wide.
\v 49 The¦352369 porch¦352370 was¦352371 eleven metres wide¦352373 and¦352377 six metres deep, \add with\add* steps¦352377 going¦352379 up¦352379 to¦352380 it¦352380 and¦352381 pillars¦352384 standing on each side of it.
\c 41
\s1 Inside the temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦352390 the man took¦352390 me¦352390 into¦352391 the¦352393 temple's¦352393 holy place and¦352394 measured¦352394 the¦352397 doorposts \add at\add* three metres thick on each side.
\v 2 The doorway¦352414 was¦352415 five metres¦352416 wide \add and\add* the¦352417 wall on each side¦352417 was¦352419 two and¦352422 a half metres¦352423 long. Then¦352425 the man measured¦352425 the dimensions of¦352426 the holy place—twenty-one metres long and eleven metres wide¦352429.
\v 3 Then¦352433 the man went¦352433 into¦352434 the \add very sacred\add* inner¦352434 place and¦352435 measured¦352435 the¦352436 door¦352438 at one metre wide, and¦352441 the¦352441 doorway¦352441 was¦352442 three metres¦352443 wide¦352444. The¦352445 walls on either side were¦352446 nearly four metres¦352447 wide.
\v 4 Then¦352449 he¦352449 measured¦352449 the square room's length¦352452 and¦352455 width¦352455 of eleven metres, \add ≡measuring\add* to the¦352461 temple¦352461 hallway. Then¦352462 he¦352462 said¦352462 to¦352463 me¦352463, “This¦352464 is¦352465 the most sacred area.”
\s1 The temple rooms
\rem /s1 The Rooms Built against the Temple Walls
\p
\v 5 Then¦352468 the man measured¦352468 the¦352469 wall¦352469 of¦352469 the¦352471 house¦352471 at three metres¦352473 thick. The¦352474 width¦352474 of¦352474 each side room around the¦352475 house was¦352476 two metres¦352477.
\v 6 There were¦352489 side rooms on three¦352489 levels, one room above another, thirty¦352490 rooms¦352497 on each level. There were ledges around the temple wall to¦352501 support¦352502 all the side rooms, as the supports¦352506 weren't built into¦352507 the¦352507 temple wall¦352507.
\v 7 \add ≈So¦352510\add* the side rooms widened and¦352511 went around going up, because¦352515 the house¦352518 went around¦352521 higher and higher all¦352523 around¦352523—the¦352528 rooms widened \add ≈on each higher level\add*, and¦352532 a stairway went up through the¦352533 middle level to¦352535 the¦352537 highest level.
\v 8 Then¦352540 I¦352540 saw¦352540 a raised part all around the temple¦352541, the¦352546 \add #foundation¦352546\add* for the¦352548 side rooms¦352548. It measured a¦352553 full rod in height¦352542—\add ≈just over three metres¦352552\add*.
\rem /s1 The Building on the West
\v 9 The¦352555 width¦352555 of¦352555 the¦352556 wall¦352556 of the side rooms on the¦352562 outside¦352562 was¦352563 two and¦352565 a half metres¦352564. There was¦352566 an open space¦352566 outside those rooms in the¦352568 temple¦352570.
\v 10 On the other side of that open space were the \add priests'\add* outer side rooms¦352573. That space was¦352575 eleven metres wide¦352574 all around¦352577 the temple¦352578.
\v 11 There were¦352585 doors into¦352585 the side rooms from another open space¦352585 with one¦352587 doorway on the¦352589 north¦352589 side, and¦352590 the other on the south¦352592. The¦352593 width¦352593 of that open area was¦352596 two and a half metres¦352597 all around.
\s1 The western side
\p
\v 12 The¦352602 building that¦352603 faced¦352606 the¦352607 courtyard on the¦352608 western side¦352608 was¦352613 thirty-six metres wide. Its wall¦352615 was¦352617 two and a half metres¦352619 thick all around, and¦352624 it was¦352625 forty-seven metres long¦352624.
\s1 The temple building measurements
\rem /s1 The Total Measurements of the Temple Building
\p
\v 13 Then¦352628 the man measured¦352628 the¦352631 temple¦352631 as being¦352633 fifty-four metres long. The¦352635 separate¦352635 building, its \add #wall¦352637\add*, and¦352636 the¦352636 courtyard \add combined\add* also¦352637 \add ≡measured¦352628\add* fifty-four metres \add ≈across\add*.
\v 14 The¦352642 width¦352642 of¦352642 the¦352643 front¦352643 of¦352643 the¦352644 courtyard in front of the¦352645 temple¦352644 was¦352647 also fifty-four metres.
\p
\v 15 Then¦352650 the man measured¦352650 the¦352651 length¦352651 of¦352651 the building behind¦352661 the temple¦352668, to its west, and¦352665 including¦352668 the¦352668 galleries¦352662 on either side, it came to fifty-four metres.
\rem /s1 Details of the Temple Building
\p The¦352669 sacred inner¦352669 place¦352657 and¦352670 the¦352670 porch¦352670,
\v 16 the¦352673 inner walls and¦352674 the¦352674 windows¦352674, including the¦352675 narrow¦352675 windows, and¦352676 the¦352676 galleries¦352676 all¦352678 around¦352678 on three¦352679 levels, were all panelled¦352682 in wood¦352683.
\v 17 Above¦352694 the¦352697 entryway¦352697 to¦352698 the¦352701 inner¦352701 sanctuary and spaced along the¦352707 \add #walls¦352707\add* there was a \add ≈repeating\add* pattern.
\v 18 It was¦352715 decorated with winged creatures and¦352717 palm¦352717 trees¦352717—with a¦352718 palm¦352718 tree¦352718 between¦352719 each winged creature, and each winged creature had two¦352723 faces¦352724:
\v 19 the¦352727 human¦352728 face¦352727 looked toward a¦352728 palm¦352731 tree¦352731 on one side, and¦352733 the¦352733 young¦352735 lion¦352735 face looked toward a palm¦352738 tree¦352738 on the other side. They were¦352740 carved all¦352746 around¦352746 the entire¦352748 temple¦352745.
\v 20 From¦352750 the¦352750 ground¦352750 to¦352751 above the¦352754 doorway¦352754, winged creatures and¦352756 palm¦352756 trees¦352756 were¦352757 carved on the¦352758 outer wall¦352758 of¦352758 the¦352759 temple¦352759.
\v 21 The door posts of the \add ≈main temple¦352762 room¦352766\add* were square¦352764, all with the¦352767 same appearance.
\rem /s1 The Wooden Altar
\v 22 The¦352770 wooden altar¦352770 was¦352771 one metre on each side¦352782 and stood one and a half metres¦352773 high¦352774. Its¦352775 corner¦352779 posts, base, and¦352781 frame were¦352783 \add all\add* made of wood¦352783.
\p Then¦352784 the man said¦352784 to¦352785 me¦352785, “This¦352786 is¦352787 the¦352787 table¦352787 that¦352788 stands \add ≈in¦352789 Yahweh's¦352790 presence¦352789\add*.”
\s1 The temple doorways
\rem /s1 The Doors
\p
\v 23 Both¦352792 the main room and¦352795 the sacred place¦352795 had double doors¦352793—
\v 24 each consisting of¦352806 two parts that swung on hinges.
\v 25 There were¦352810 carvings of¦352814 winged creatures and¦352817 palm¦352817 trees¦352817 on the¦352814 doors¦352814, just¦352818 like¦352818 on the walls¦352820, and¦352821 there was a¦352821 wooden¦352822 roof over the¦352825 front¦352825 porch¦352826.
\v 26 There were narrow¦352830 windows¦352829 and¦352831 palm¦352831 trees¦352831 on either \add #side¦352836\add* of¦352836 the¦352837 porch¦352837, like the¦352838 side rooms¦352838 of¦352838 the¦352839 temple¦352839, and¦352840 they also had overhanging roofs.
\c 42
\s1 Two buildings near the temple
\rem /s1 Rooms for the Priests; The Holy Chambers and the Outer Wall; Two Buildings Near the Temple
\p
\v 1 Next the man sent me¦352842 out¦352842 to¦352843 the outer courtyard on the northern¦352849 side, and¦352850 he¦352850 brought¦352850 me¦352850 to¦352851 \add #rooms¦352853\add* \add ≈in¦352855 front¦352855 of¦352855\add* the¦352856 outer courtyard and¦352857 the northern¦352863 outer wall.
\v 2 That building with a north-facing¦352873 entrance¦352872 was fifty-four metres long¦352869 and¦352874 twenty-seven metres wide¦352874.
\v 3 Some of those rooms faced¦352891 the¦352879 inner¦352882 courtyard¦352881 and were eleven metres away from the¦352882 sanctuary. There were three levels of rooms, and¦352883 the¦352884 ones above looked down on the¦352887 ones below and were¦352889 open to¦352889 them, having a walkway. Some of the rooms looked out onto the outer¦352887 courtyard.
\v 4 A five metre wide¦352901 passage¦352898 ran \add ≈in¦352896 front¦352896 of¦352896\add* the¦352897 rooms¦352897 and was fifty-four\f + \fr 42:4 \ft The Hebrew text seemed to be missing a word here (as it didn't make sense), so the word from the (approx. 300 B.C.) Septuagint translation was used.\f* metres¦352900 long. The¦352904 rooms' doors¦352908 \add ≈faced\add* north¦352909.
\v 5 But¦352911 the¦352912 upper¦352912 halls were¦352913 smaller, because¦352914 the walkways \add ≈there used\add* more space than they¦352916 did in the¦352919 bottom and¦352920 middle¦352920 levels of the¦352921 building.
\v 6 \add @The third storey halls\add* had no pillars (unlike the¦352930 courtyards¦352930), so¦352933 \add @the¦352934 rooms¦352934 at¦352934 the¦352934 highest¦352934 level¦352934\add* were¦352934 smaller than¦352935 those on the¦352935 bottom and¦352936 middle¦352936 levels.
\v 7 The outside¦352942 wall¦352939 ran along the¦352944 rooms¦352944 toward the¦352947 outer¦352947 courtyard¦352946 that¦352940 was¦352943 in front¦352950 of¦352950 the¦352951 rooms¦352951, \add and\add* was¦352953 twenty-seven metres long¦352952.
\v 8 The \add row of\add* rooms¦352959 along the¦352962 outer¦352962 courtyard¦352961 was¦352963 twenty-seven metres long, and¦352965 the¦352968 \add ≡row of rooms\add* facing¦352968 the¦352969 sanctuary was fifty-four metres long.
\v 9 There was an entrance to the lowest rooms from¦352979 the¦352979 eastern¦352979 side, coming¦352980 from¦352982 the¦352983 outer¦352983 courtyard¦352982.
\v 10 There was¦352985 \add also a set of\add* rooms¦352999 along the outer courtyard wall¦352987 on the eastern¦352990 side of¦352993 the outer courtyard, in front¦352997 of¦352997 the¦352998 sanctuary's inner courtyard,
\v 11 with a¦353001 walkway \add ≈in¦353002 front¦353002 of¦353002\add* them¦353002. They \add ≈looked similar¦353003 to\add* the¦353004 rooms¦353004 on the¦353006 northern¦353007 side and had the¦353007 same \add ≈measurements\add*, as well as the same exits¦353012 and¦353013 arrangements¦353013 and¦353014 doors¦353014.
\v 12 There were also¦353016 doorways into rooms¦353017 on the¦353020 south¦353020 side that¦353018 were¦353019 similar¦353016 to what was on the¦353016 north side. There was an inside passage with an outer door¦353021—the¦353022 passage had doors¦353016 into¦353025 all the rooms. At¦353022 the eastern¦353029 end of the passage there was a door from the outside.
\p
\v 13 Then¦353032 the man told¦353032 me¦353033, “The northern¦353035 rooms¦353036 and the¦353037 southern¦353037 rooms that¦353038 are¦353039 in front¦353041 of¦353041 the¦353042 outer courtyard \add ≈are¦353045 sacred\add* rooms¦353045 where¦353047 the¦353051 priests¦353051 who¦353052 work nearest¦353054 to¦353055 Yahweh¦353055 may eat¦353048 the¦353056 very sacred food. They'll¦353059 put¦353059 the very sacred things¦353057 there¦353058—the food offering¦353063, the¦353064 sin¦353064 offering¦353064, and¦353065 the¦353065 guilt¦353065 offering¦353065—because¦353066 it's¦353032 a \add very\add* sacred¦353068 area.
\v 14 When¦353070 the¦353071 priests¦353071 enter it, they¦353074 must¦353074 not¦353072 go¦353074 \add back\add* out¦353074 of the¦353075 sacred¦353075 area to¦353076 the¦353078 outer¦353079 courtyard¦353078, without laying aside the clothes¦353082 in which¦353083 they¦353085 served¦353085, since those \add ≡clothes¦353082\add* are¦353089 sacred¦353089. So¦353095 they¦353095 must¦353095 dress in other¦353094 clothes before going \add ≈back into¦353096 the public areas\add*.”
\s1 Measuring outside the temple
\rem /s1 The Measurements of the Temple Area
\p
\v 15 The man completed¦353101 measuring¦353104 the¦353105 temple¦353105 interior¦353106 and¦353107 then took¦353107 me¦353107 out¦353107 to the eastern¦353113 gate¦353109 and¦353114 measured¦353114 all the surrounding area there.
\v 16-19 He¦353119 measured¦353119 each side¦353120 \add of the wall\add* with¦353122 \add &his\add* \add ≈measuring\add* rod, \add and it was\add* 270m\f + \fr 42:16 \ft Following the Septuagint here, which seems more accurate (unless he now has a shorter measuring rod).\f* long on each¦353131 of¦353134 the eastern¦353121, northern¦353135, southern¦353146, and western sides¦353158.
\v 20 \add ≈So\add* the four¦353168 sides¦353169 that were measured¦353170 (and thus the wall¦353171 around it) was¦353177 270m on each side, and¦353179 the¦353179 wall separated¦353182 the¦353184 sacred¦353184 area from the \add ≈public area\add*.
\c 43
\s1 God's powerful splendour of God at the temple
\rem /s1 The Lord Returns to the Temple; The Divine Glory Returns to the Temple; The Lord's Glory Returns; The Glory Returns to the Temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦353187 the man took¦353187 me¦353187 to¦353188 the gate that¦353192 opened to the¦353195 east¦353195.
\v 2 Wow¦353197! The¦353198 splendour¦353198 of¦353198 Yisrael's¦353200 \add !(Israel¦353200)\add* god¦353199 \add ≈appeared towards\add* the¦353203 east¦353203. His voice¦353204 was¦353205 like¦353205 the¦353205 \add ≈roar of¦353205 rushing water¦353206\add*, and¦353208 the¦353208 land¦353208 shone with his¦353210 \add ≈brilliance\add*.\x + \xo 43:2: \xt Eze 10:3-4,18-19; 11:22-23; Rev 1:15.\x*
\v 3 \add ≈What I¦353215 saw¦353215 in this vision was¦353216 like¦353216 what I'd¦353219 seen¦353219 in my earlier\add* visions¦353225, first by the¦353231 Kevar¦353233 Canal, then later when¦353220 he\f + \fr 43:3 \ft It's only one letter difference, but the Hebrew has ‘I’ here which seems wrong and is adjusted in some later manuscripts.\f* came¦353220 to¦353221 destroy¦353221 \add @Yerushalem¦353228\add* \add !(Jerusalem¦353228)\add*. I¦353234 fell¦353234 to¦353235 \add my knees with\add* my¦353237 face¦353237 \add to the ground\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈So¦353239\add* Yahweh's¦353240 \add ≈brightness¦353239\add* entered into¦353242 the¦353244 temple¦353244 via the eastern¦353250 entrance¦353246.
\p
\v 5 Then¦353252 the spirit¦353253 lifted¦353252 me¦353252 up¦353252 and¦353254 brought¦353254 me¦353254 into¦353255 the¦353258 inner¦353258 court¦353257, and¦353259 wow¦353259, Yahweh's¦353263 brilliance was filling¦353260 the¦353264 temple¦353264.
\v 6 ⇔Then¦353270 as the man¦353270 was¦353271 standing¦353272 \add there\add* beside¦353273 me¦353273, I¦353266 heard¦353266 someone¦353267 speak¦353267 to¦353268 me¦353268 from¦353269 inside the¦353269 temple¦353269,
\v 7 and¦353275 \add @the¦353275 voice¦353275\add* told¦353275 me¦353276, “Son¦353277 of¦353277 humanity¦353279, this is the¦353282 place¦353282 \add where I'll sit on\add* my¦353283 throne¦353283 and¦353284 \add ≈rest\add* my¦353288 feet¦353288—where¦353289 I'll¦353290 live¦353290 in¦353293 the¦353293 middle¦353293 of the¦353294 Israeli people¦353294 forever¦353297. Yisrael's¦353296 \add ≈descendants\add* and¦353298 their kings will¦353299 no longer profane my¦353305 \add ≈sacred¦353305\add* name¦353304 with their faithlessness or with the¦353309 corpses¦353309 of¦353309 their¦353310 kings¦353310 at their¦353311 \add ≈hilltop¦353311 shrines¦353311\add*.
\v 8 They'll no longer profane my \add ≈sacred\add* name by putting¦353313 their¦353314 \add ≈entrance\add* next to my¦353317 \add ≈entrance\add*, and¦353318 their¦353318 doorposts¦353320 next to my¦353320 doorposts, with nothing but¦353321 a wall¦353321 between¦353322 me¦353322 and¦353323 them¦353323. They¦353324 profaned my¦353329 \add ≈sacred¦353329\add* name with their¦353330 disgusting actions, so¦353333 I¦353333 \add ≈destroyed\add* them¦353334 with my¦353335 anger¦353335.
\v 9 Now¦353337 they're to remove their¦353341 prostitution¦353341 and¦353342 \add ?their kings' corpses\add* from¦353344 in front of me¦353344, and¦353345 I'll¦353345 live¦353345 \add ≈among¦353346 them¦353346\add* forever¦353347.
\p
\v 10 “Son¦353351 of¦353351 humanity¦353353, \add ≈describe that¦353363 temple to the Israeli¦353359 people\add* so¦353363 they'll¦353363 be¦353363 ashamed¦353363 of¦353364 their¦353364 \add ≈disobedience\add* and¦353365 \add ?≈think about those plans\add*.
\v 11 If¦353370 they're¦353372 ashamed¦353372 of everything they've¦353376 done¦353376, then reveal the¦353377 design¦353377 of¦353377 that temple¦353378 to them—its¦353379 details, its¦353380 entrances¦353381 and¦353382 exits¦353380, its¦353384 complete design, and¦353386 all¦353387 its¦353389 decrees and¦353390 rules. Then¦353394 \add right\add* \add ≈in front of¦353396 them¦353399,\add* write¦353400 that down so¦353402 they'll¦353402 see its complete design and¦353408 all¦353410 its¦353412 rules, and¦353413 then obey them¦353414.
\v 12 This¦353416 is¦353417 the¦353417 regulation for the¦353418 temple¦353418: From the¦353421 peak¦353421 of¦353421 the¦353422 hill¦353422 to all¦353423 the surrounding border¦353425 around it, it'll be¦353429 very sacred. Listen, that's the¦353434 regulation for the¦353435 temple¦353435.
\s1 The altar dimensions
\rem /s1 The Altar
\p
\v 13 These¦353437 are¦353438 the¦353438 altar's measurements¦353438, using the same kind of¦353438 measuring rod \add that was used for the temple area\add*: There's a¦353444 gutter¦353444 around the¦353439 altar¦353439 that's \add both\add* half a metre deep and¦353443 wide¦353443. There's a¦353446 rim around¦353453 it that's 23cm wide. That'll be¦353457 the¦353457 base for \add the rest of\add* the¦353458 altar¦353458.\x + \xo 43:13-17: \xt Exo 27:1-2; 2Ch 4:1.\x*
\v 14 The¦353460 lower part of¦353460 the¦353461 gutter¦353460 is one¦353470 metre high to¦353462 the¦353464 lower ledge¦353464 that surrounds the altar. The lower ledge is¦353469 half a¦353469 metre wide. From¦353471 that ledge¦353471 to¦353473 the¦353475 upper ledge, it's two¦353466 metres¦353467. That ledge¦353475 is¦353477 also¦353479 half a metre wide¦353479.
\v 15 The¦353482 altar¦353482 body \add at the top\add* is¦353483 another two metres¦353484 high and has a \add ≈hornlike projection\add* jutting up \add from each of its corners\add*.
\v 16 The sides¦353503 of¦353503 the square¦353500 body are six metres long¦353496.
\v 17 The¦353505 upper ledge¦353505 that borders¦353515 the altar body is¦353506 also a square, eight metres long¦353508 on each side¦353514, and has a rim all¦353524 around¦353516 that's 27cm wide¦353511. There's a gutter¦353520 measuring half¦353518 a metre at the bottom of¦353518 the¦353519 altar. There are¦353523 steps¦353525 leading up to¦353526 the east¦353527 side of the altar.”
\s1 The purification of the new altar
\rem /s1 The Consecration of the Altar
\p
\v 18 Then¦353529 \add @the¦353529 man¦353529\add* told¦353529 me¦353530, “Son¦353531 of¦353531 humanity¦353533, the master¦353536 Yahweh¦353537 says¦353535 that those¦353538 are¦353539 the¦353539 dimensions for when they make¦353542 the¦353540 altar¦353540 for presenting the burnt¦353545 offering¦353545 on, and¦353546 for sprinkling¦353546 blood¦353548 onto.\x + \xo 43:18-27: \xt Exo 29:35-37.\x*
\v 19 You¦353550 must¦353550 give¦353550 a¦353565 bull¦353565 from the¦353568 cattle as a¦353569 sin¦353569 offering¦353569 for the Levitical priests¦353553 who¦353555 are¦353557 Tsadok's¦353558 descendants¦353557—those who approach¦353559 me to¦353560 serve¦353564 me¦353560. That's the¦353561 master¦353562 Yahweh's¦353563 declaration¦353561.
\v 20 Then¦353571 you'll¦353571 take¦353571 some¦353572 of¦353572 \add @the¦353572 bull's¦353572\add* blood¦353572 and¦353573 place it on¦353574 the¦353576 altar's ‘horns¦353577’ and¦353578 on the¦353580 four¦353580 top edges and¦353583 the¦353585 surrounding¦353586 rim. You'll¦353587 purify¦353587 it¦353588 and¦353589 \add ≈make¦353589 it¦353589 allowable in my presence\add*.
\v 21 Then¦353591 take¦353591 that sin¦353594 offering¦353594 bull¦353593 and¦353595 burn¦353595 it¦353595 in¦353596 the¦353596 designated part of¦353596 the¦353597 temple¦353597 area outside¦353598 the sanctuary¦353599.
\v 22 Then¦353601 on¦353601 the¦353602 second¦353602 day¦353601, you'll¦353603 get from the goats¦353606 a¦353604 male¦353604 goat¦353604 without any blemishes, and offer it as a¦353608 sin¦353608 offering¦353608—the priests will¦353609 purify¦353609 the¦353612 altar¦353612 just¦353613 as¦353613 they¦353614 purified¦353614 it with the bull¦353615.
\v 23 When¦353617 you finish¦353617 that purification¦353618, offer an unblemished bull¦353620 from the¦353623 cattle and¦353625 an unblemished ram¦353625 from¦353626 the¦353628 flock¦353628,
\v 24 and¦353631 offer¦353638 them¦353631 to¦353632 \add me,\add* Yahweh¦353633. The¦353635 priests¦353635 will¦353634 throw¦353634 salt¦353637 onto them and present them¦353639 as a¦353640 burnt¦353640 offering¦353640 to¦353641 Yahweh¦353641.
\v 25 Then every day¦353649 for \add the next\add* seven¦353643 days¦353644 you¦353645 must¦353645 bring a¦353646 billy goat¦353646 \add to the priest\add* to be sacrificed for an offering¦353648, \add so I'll accept the altar\add*. You must also¦353650 sacrifice a¦353650 young¦353650 bull¦353650 and¦353654 a¦353654 ram¦353654, each without any defect, that the¦353657 priests will¦353659 provide.
\v 26 They¦353663 \add ≈must¦353663 atone¦353663\add* for¦353663 the¦353666 altar¦353666 for seven¦353661 days¦353662 and¦353667 purify¦353667 it¦353668, and¦353669 in that way they must consecrate it \add to me\add*.
\v 27 \add ≈After doing all that\add*, from the¦353680 eighth¦353680 day¦353679 onward¦353681 the¦353683 priests¦353683 will¦353682 prepare you \add people\add*'s burnt¦353689 offerings and your¦353692 peace¦353692 offerings on the¦353686 altar¦353686, and¦353693 I'll¦353693 accept¦353693 you¦353694. That's the¦353695 master¦353696 Yahweh's¦353697 declaration¦353695.”
\c 44
\s1 The locked gate
\rem /s1 The Prince, the Levites, the Priests; The Use of the East Gate; The Prince, Levites, and Priests; The Closed Gate
\p
\v 1 Then¦353700 the man took¦353700 me back¦353700 to the¦353705 outer¦353705 sanctuary¦353704 gate¦353703 facing¦353706 east¦353707, \add ≈but¦353708\add* it was¦353709 shut¦353709,
\v 2 and¦353711 Yahweh told¦353711 me¦353712, “This¦353715 gate¦353714 has been sealed shut¦353716—it¦353717 won't¦353718 be¦353719 opened¦353719. No person will¦353723 go¦353723 through it¦353724, because¦353725 Yisrael's¦353729 \add !(Israel¦353729)\add* god¦353727 Yahweh entered through it¦353731, so¦353732 it's¦353732 \add now\add* closed¦353733.
\v 3 \add Only\add* \add &Yisrael's¦353737\add* ruler will¦353740 sit¦353740 by it¦353742 to¦353743 eat¦353743 food¦353745 \add ≈in¦353748 front¦353748 of¦353748\add* Yahweh¦353749, \add but\add* he'll enter via the¦353752 gate's¦353752 porch¦353751, and¦353754 leave¦353755 the same way¦353754.”
\s1 Foreigners disallowed in the temple
\rem /s1 Rules for Admission to the Temple; Admission to the Temple
\p
\v 4 Then¦353757 he¦353757 took¦353757 me¦353757 to the¦353758 front of¦353758 the¦353760 temple by way¦353758 of¦353760 the¦353761 northern¦353761 gate¦353760, and¦353766 I¦353766 looked¦353766, and¦353767 wow¦353767, Yahweh's¦353771 brilliance filled¦353768 \add *his¦353771\add* temple¦353774, and¦353776 I¦353776 fell¦353776 to¦353777 \add my knees with\add* my¦353779 face \add bowed towards the ground\add*.
\v 5 Then¦353781 Yahweh told¦353781 me¦353782, “Son¦353784 of¦353784 humanity¦353786, \add ≈pay attention\add* and¦353789 look¦353789 with¦353790 your¦353790 eyes¦353790 and¦353791 listen¦353792 with¦353791 your¦353791 ears¦353791 to everything that¦353796 I'm¦353797 declaring to you: all¦353800 the¦353802 instructions¦353802 about Yahweh's¦353805 temple and¦353806 all¦353806 its¦353808 regulations¦353802. \add ≈Take special note of¦353811\add* the¦353812 temple's entrance¦353812 and exits¦353815.
\v 6 Then¦353818 tell¦353818 the¦353824 rebellious¦353821 \add ≈Israelis¦353825\add* that the master¦353828 Yahweh¦353829 says¦353827 this: Let all¦353833 your disgusting actions be enough¦353830 for¦353832 you¦353832 Israeli¦353837 people—
\v 7 that you brought \add ≈foreigners¦353842\add* with uncircumcised¦353843 \add #hearts¦353845\add* and¦353846 uncircumcised¦353846 flesh¦353847 to¦353848 be¦353848 in¦353849 my¦353849 sanctuary¦353849, profaning¦353850 my¦353853 temple¦353853, while you¦353854 \add people\add* were offering me food¦353857, fat¦353858, and¦353859 blood¦353859. \add %You¦353860\add* \add people\add* have broken¦353860 my¦353863 agreement¦353863 with all¦353865 your¦353867 disgusting actions.
\v 8 You¦353870 haven't carried out your duties regarding my¦353872 sacred¦353872 things¦353872, but¦353873 \add instead,\add* you¦353873 \add people\add* appointed¦353873 others to take up your duties, and you assigned them¦353873 to¦353874 care for¦353877 my sacred place.
\p
\v 9 “The master¦353882 Yahweh¦353883 says¦353881 this: No \add ≈foreigner¦353888\add*, from¦353898 any¦353898 of¦353900 those who¦353903 live among the¦353904 Israeli¦353906 people¦353905, may enter my sacred place if they're not physically¦353892 circumcised or if they \add ≈haven't declared themself to¦353895 be one of¦353897 my¦353897 people\add*.
\s1 Demotion of the Levites
\rem /s1 The Levites Are Excluded from the Priesthood
\p
\v 10 The¦353911 Levites¦353911 went far from¦353914 me¦353914—wandering away from¦353919 me¦353919 \add and\add* going after¦353920 their¦353921 idols¦353921, but¦353922 they'll¦353922 be punished.
\v 11 They're¦353925 \add still\add* servants in¦353926 my¦353926 sanctuary¦353926, watching the¦353931 temple gates¦353931 and¦353933 serving¦353933 inside, and they slaughter¦353938 the¦353941 burnt¦353941 \add #offerings¦353941\add* and¦353942 the¦353944 people's¦353945 \add #sacrifices¦353944\add*, and¦353946 they'll¦353947 stand¦353947 \add ≈in¦353948 front¦353948 of¦353948\add* the people and serve¦353949 them¦353949.
\v 12 \add However,\add* because¦353951 they¦353953 sacrificed to¦353955 their¦353956 idols¦353956, they¦353957 became sinful stumbling¦353961 \add #blocks¦353961\add* for¦353958 the¦353958 \add ≈Israeli¦353960 people\add*, therefore¦353963 I've¦353966 \add ≈promised to take action\add* against them¦353968, and¦353972 they'll¦353972 \add ≈pay for their disobedience\add*. That's¦353952 the¦353969 master¦353970 Yahweh's¦353971 declaration¦353969.
\v 13 They¦353977 mustn't come near¦353977 to¦353978 me¦353978 to¦353979 act as¦353979 my priests¦353979 or to approach¦353981 any of my¦353986 sacred things¦353989, the¦353990 most sacred¦353990 things¦353990. Instead, they'll¦353991 bear¦353991 their¦353992 shame¦353992 and¦353993 their¦353993 guilt for the disgusting things they've¦353995 done¦353995,
\v 14 but¦353997 I'll¦353997 place them¦353998 as housekeepers in the temple¦354001—for all¦354002 its¦354003 \add mundane\add* duties and¦354004 \add ≈all¦354004 the menial work¦354003\add* that's¦354005 done¦354006 in¦354007 it¦354007.
\s1 Tsadok's descendants as priests
\rem /s1 The Levitical Priests; The Priests
\p
\v 15 Then¦354010 the¦354010 Levitical priests¦354010, those descendants¦354012 of¦354012 Tsadok¦354013 who¦354014 fulfilled the¦354018 duties of¦354018 my¦354019 sanctuary¦354019 when¦354020 the¦354021 Israeli¦354023 people¦354021 were wandering away from¦354024 me¦354024, they'll¦354026 approach¦354026 me¦354027 to¦354028 worship¦354028 me¦354028. They'll¦354029 stand¦354029 in front¦354030 of me to offer me¦354032 the fat¦354033 and¦354034 the blood¦354034. That's the¦354035 master¦354036 Yahweh's¦354037 declaration¦354035.
\v 16 They'll¦354040 come¦354040 to¦354041 my¦354043 sanctuary¦354043 and¦354044 approach¦354045 my¦354048 table¦354048 to¦354049 worship¦354049 me¦354049 and¦354050 to fulfill their duties to me.
\v 17 When¦354056 they come¦354056 to¦354057 the¦354059 gates¦354059 of¦354059 the inner courtyard, they'll have to dress in linen¦354063 clothing,\x + \xo 44:17-18: \xt Exo 28:39-43; Lev 16:4.\x* because they¦354064 mustn't \add ≈wear\add* wool¦354069 inside¦354074 the courtyard gates or in the temple.
\v 18 \add ≈They¦354078 must¦354078 wear\add* linen turbans¦354076 on¦354079 their¦354081 \add #heads¦354081\add* and¦354082 linen¦354083 underclothes¦354082 around their¦354087 waists¦354087. They¦354089 mustn't dress in clothing that makes them sweat¦354090.
\v 19 When¦354092 they¦354092 go¦354092 \add back\add* out¦354092 to¦354093 the¦354096 outer¦354096 courtyard¦354099 to go \add back again\add* to the¦354103 people¦354103, they¦354104 must¦354104 take off¦354104 the clothes¦354107 they wore when they served¦354111—they must take them¦354112 off and¦354113 lay¦354113 them down¦354113 in¦354115 a \add ≈sacred\add* \add #room¦354115\add*,\x + \xo 44:19: \xt Lev 16:23.\x* so¦354117 they¦354117 don't¦354120 make other¦354119 people¦354125 sacred by¦354126 contact with their¦354126 special clothing.
\p
\v 20 \add @The priests\add* mustn't shave¦354130 their heads or allow¦354133 \add &their\add* hair¦354131 to hang loosely, but they¦354135 must¦354135 \add ≈keep the hair on their heads neatly trimmed\add*.
\v 21 No priest¦354146 should drink¦354143 wine¦354140 \add ≈before\add* he comes¦354147 to¦354148 the¦354151 inner¦354151 court¦354150.\x + \xo 44:21: \xt Lev 10:9.\x*
\v 22 \add ≈Also¦354153, priests can't¦354155 marry widows¦354153 of men who¦354168 weren't priests, or women who've been divorced¦354154—they're¦354157 permitted to marry only Israeli¦354166 women who've never been¦354169 married, or widows¦354170 of¦354171 other priests¦354171\add*.
\p
\v 23 They¦354177 must¦354177 teach¦354177 the people¦354176 the difference between¦354178 things¦354179 that are sacred¦354179 and¦354180 things¦354180 that are not, and teach them how to know¦354185 what things are acceptable to¦354185 me and what things are not.\x + \xo 44:23: \xt Lev 10:10.\x*
\v 24 In a¦354189 dispute¦354189 they must¦354191 judge¦354195 in accordance with my¦354194 instructions and¦354198 regulations¦354203. They must keep my¦354200 \add #law¦354200\add* and¦354201 my statutes at every¦354204 religious celebration, and they¦354207 must¦354207 observe¦354207 my¦354210 sacred rest days.
\p
\v 25 \add @Priests\add* mustn't defile themselves by going¦354218 near¦354213 a¦354216 person's¦354216 dead body. However, if the dead person was \add &his¦354223\add* father¦354223 or mother¦354224, son¦354225 or daughter¦354226, or brother¦354227 or unmarried sister¦354228, then they¦354235 can defile themselves¦354235,
\v 26 and¦354237 after¦354237 they've \add ≈been purified¦354238\add*, they must \add ≈wait\add* for seven¦354239 days¦354240.
\v 27 Then \add after that\add*, \add ≈when\add* he goes¦354246 into the¦354253 inner¦354253 courtyard¦354252 to¦354254 serve¦354254 in¦354255 the sacred¦354249 place¦354255, he¦354256 must¦354256 bring¦354256 a sin¦354257 offering¦354257 for himself. That's the¦354258 master¦354259 Yahweh's¦354260 declaration¦354258.
\p
\v 28 \add ≈The priests must¦354262 not own any land\add*—I¦354265 am¦354266 their¦354266 inheritance and¦354267 I'll¦354273 be¦354274 their¦354274 property.\x + \xo 44:28: \xt Num 18:20.\x*
\v 29 They'll¦354280 have the¦354276 food offerings¦354276, the¦354277 sin¦354277 offerings¦354277, and¦354278 the¦354278 guilt¦354278 offerings¦354278 to eat¦354280, plus¦354281 everything in¦354284 Yisrael¦354284 \add !(Israel¦354284)\add* that's devoted¦354283 to¦354285 Yahweh will¦354286 be theirs.\x + \xo 44:29-30: \xt Num 18:8-19.\x*
\v 30 The¦354288 best¦354288 of¦354288 the¦354291 firstfruits¦354291 \add ≈from every¦354292 harvest\add* and¦354293 every¦354293 contribution¦354295 \add from the people\add* will¦354300 belong¦354300 to the priests, and you¦354303 \add Israelis\add* will¦354303 give¦354303 the¦354301 first¦354301 of¦354301 your¦354302 \add bread\add* dough¦354302\f + \fr 44:30 \ft Probably after it's cooked???\f* to¦354304 the priests¦354304 so that blessing¦354306 may rest¦354305 on your¦354309 home¦354309.
\v 31 Priests¦354323 must not¦354321 eat¦354322 \add ≈the¦354323 meat of any bird¦354317 or animal that's found dead or that's been killed by any other\add* animal.\x + \xo 44:31: \xt Lev 22:8.\x*
\c 45
\s1 Yahweh's section of land
\rem /s1 Division of the Land; The Holy District; The Lord's Portion of the Country
\p
\v 1 When¦354326 you¦354326 throw dice to divide up¦354331 the¦354329 land¦354329 as an¦354330 inheritance¦354330 \add for the tribes\add*, you¦354331 must¦354331 make¦354326 an offering¦354331 of land to¦354333 Yahweh¦354333. That offering will become a¦354335 sacred¦354335 part¦354335 of the¦354338 land¦354338: \add ≈thirteen kilometres by ten¦354345 kilometres in size\add*.\f + \fr 45:1 \ft The ‘ten kilometres’ (twenty thousand cubits) from the Septuagint appears to correct the Hebrew which has half of that, but then v3 and v4 wouldn't make sense.\f* \add ≈That entire area\add* will become sacred.
\v 2 \add ≈Within that¦354356 area,\add* a¦354364 270m wide square¦354364 area that'll surround the¦354359 sacred¦354359 place¦354359 \add ≈will be left empty\add*, with a further border around¦354365 it¦354369 twenty-seven metres wide.
\v 3 Inside that¦354375 \add first, larger\add* area¦354374, measure¦354376 a¦354377 \add rectangle\add* thirteen kilometres by five¦354378 kilometres for the¦354388 sanctuary¦354388—the most sacred¦354389 place¦354389.
\v 4 It'll¦354400 be¦354392 a¦354392 sacred¦354392 place in the¦354395 nation¦354395 for the priests¦354397 who¦354398 serve¦354398 Yahweh, who come¦354401 near¦354401 Yahweh to¦354402 serve¦354402 him. It'll¦354406 be¦354406 a¦354408 place¦354408 for¦354409 \add &their\add* houses¦354409 and¦354410 a¦354410 sacred area for the sacred place.
\v 5 The \add other half\add*, which is \add also\add* thirteen kilometres long¦354416 and¦354413 five¦354413 kilometres wide¦354419, will¦354420 be for towns for the Levites¦354422 who¦354423 serve¦354423 in the¦354424 temple¦354424.
\p
\v 6 You'll¦354432 designate an area thirteen kilometres long¦354436 by half a kilometre wide¦354435 for the¦354431 city¦354431 that will¦354432 be next to¦354440 the¦354441 area reserved for the¦354442 holy place. This city will¦354447 belong¦354447 to¦354443 all¦354443 the¦354445 \add ≈Israeli¦354446 people\add*.
\s1 The leader's section of land
\rem /s1 Land for the Prince
\p
\v 7 The leader's land will be on both sides of the area reserved for the holy place and the city—on the¦354469 west¦354469 and¦354471 the¦354471 east. \add &Its\add* length¦354475 will \add ≈be the¦354478 same as those portions¦354478 that it's parallel¦354476 to¦354481\add*.
\v 8 That area will¦354487 belong¦354487 to¦354490 Yisrael's¦354491 \add !(Israel¦354491)\add* leader \add so\add* the leaders¦354496 won't¦354492 keep oppressing¦354494 my¦354496 people¦354499, \add ≈but¦354500 rather\add* they'll¦354501 give¦354501 the¦354502 rest of¦354502 the land¦354486 to¦354505 \add ≈be divided among the tribes¦354505\add*.
\rem /s1 Rules for the Prince
\v 9 The master¦354511 Yahweh¦354512 says¦354510 this: \add ≈That's plenty\add* for¦354515 you¦354515, leaders¦354516 of¦354516 Yisrael¦354517! \add ≈Replace your\add* violence¦354519 and¦354521 strife \add ≈with\add* justice¦354521 and¦354522 righteousness¦354522. Quit your¦354525 evictions¦354525 of¦354525 my¦354527 people¦354527. That's the¦354528 master¦354529 Yahweh's¦354530 declaration¦354528.
\s1 Dealing with produce and animals
\rem /s1 Weights and Measures
\p
\v 10 You \add Israelis\add* must \add ≈use accurate scales¦354532 and measuring containers\add*.\x + \xo 45:10: \xt Lev 19:36.\x*
\v 11 \add ≈The¦354544 baskets for measuring dry things and¦354545 containers for measuring¦354546 liquids (called an ‘efah’ for dry things and a ‘bath’ for liquids, both a tenth¦354553 of¦354553 the large 220 litre ‘homer’) must be the same volume—each must hold twenty-two litres\add*.
\v 12 \add ≈For weights, the¦354562 shekel¦354562 weight must be ten¦354570 grams. (Twenty¦354563 ‘gerahs¦354564’ makes one shekel, then sixty shekels makes one ‘mina¦354573’.)\add*
\rem /s1 Special Offerings and Celebrations; Offerings; Offerings and Holy Days
\p
\v 13 You¦354580 \add Israelis\add* must¦354580 present¦354585 \add to the leader\add* \add ≈one measure of wheat¦354584 or barley¦354588 for every sixty measures\add* \add that you harvest\add*,
\v 14 \add ≈one measure of¦354590 olive oil for¦354602 every one hundred measures\add* \add that you produce\add*,
\v 15 plus¦354608 one¦354610 sheep¦354608 or goat from¦354611 the¦354613 flock¦354613 for every two¦354616 hundred¦354616 animals from¦354617 the¦354617 watered regions¦354617 of¦354617 Yisrael¦354618 \add !(Israel¦354618)\add*, to be used for any burnt¦354620 offering¦354620 or peace¦354621 offering¦354621 to¦354622 make¦354622 atonement¦354622 for the people. That's the master¦354625 Yahweh's¦354626 declaration¦354624.
\p
\v 16 All¦354628 the¦354629 people¦354629 in the¦354630 country¦354630 will¦354631 give that¦354635 contribution¦354634 to Yisrael's¦354637 leader¦354636.
\v 17 It'll¦354642 be¦354642 the leader's responsibility to supply the animals for the¦354643 burnt¦354643 offerings¦354643, the¦354644 grain¦354644 offerings¦354644, and¦354645 the¦354645 drink¦354645 offerings¦354645 at¦354646 the festivals¦354646 and¦354647 the new moon¦354647 celebrations, and¦354648 on¦354648 the rest days—all¦354649 the¦354651 \add ≈Israeli¦354653 people's\add* scheduled festivals. He'll¦354656 provide¦354656 for the¦354659 sin¦354659 offerings¦354659, the¦354662 grain¦354662 offerings¦354662, the¦354665 burnt¦354665 offerings¦354665, and¦354666 the¦354668 peace¦354668 offerings¦354668 \add ≈to¦354669 make¦354669 the¦354671 Israeli¦354673 people right\add* \add with God\add*.
\s1 The festivals
\rem /s1 Festivals; The Festivals
\r (Exo. 12:1-20; Lev. 23:33-43)
\p
\v 18 The master¦354679 Yahweh¦354680 says¦354678 this: On¦354682 the first \add day\add* of¦354683 the first month¦354683 \add of the year\add*, you¦354684 must¦354684 take¦354684 an unblemished¦354690 bull¦354685 from the¦354689 herd¦354689 and¦354691 perform a sin¦354691 offering \add to purify\add* the¦354694 temple.
\v 19 The¦354697 priest¦354697 will¦354696 take¦354696 some¦354698 of¦354698 the¦354698 blood¦354698 of¦354698 that sin¦354699 offering¦354699 and¦354700 place it¦354700 on the¦354703 doorposts¦354703 of¦354703 the¦354704 temple¦354704 and¦354705 on the¦354707 four¦354707 corners¦354708 of¦354708 the¦354709 border of¦354710 the altar¦354710, and¦354711 on¦354711 the¦354713 doorposts¦354713 of¦354713 the¦354714 gate¦354714 to the¦354716 inner¦354716 court¦354715.
\v 20 You'll¦354719 do¦354719 this \add again\add* on¦354720 the seventh \add day\add* of that month¦354721 for each person's¦354724 accidental sin, or sin by ignorance. \add In that way you'll\add* \add ≈make¦354725 the¦354728 temple¦354728 pure\add* \add in God's sight\add*.
\p
\v 21 Then on¦354731 the fourteenth day¦354733 of¦354734 that first month¦354734, you¦354736 \add Israelis\add* will have a¦354738 seven-day celebration¦354738, \add ≈when you'll eat¦354742 bread¦354741 made without any raising agent\add*.\x + \xo 45:21: \xt Exo 12:1-20; Num 28:16-25.\x*
\v 22 That¦354747 same day¦354746, the leader¦354745 will¦354744 prepare a¦354754 bull¦354754 as a¦354755 sin¦354755 offering¦354755 for¦354748 himself¦354748 and¦354749 for¦354749 all¦354750 the¦354752 people¦354752 in the¦354753 country¦354753.
\v 23 For the¦354757 seven¦354757 days¦354758 of¦354758 the¦354760 celebration¦354760, the leader will¦354761 prepare a¦354762 burnt¦354762 offering¦354762 for¦354763 Yahweh¦354763: Seven¦354764 bulls¦354765 and¦354766 seven¦354766 unblemished¦354768 rams¦354767 each day¦354769 for seven¦354770 days¦354771, and¦354772 a¦354773 male¦354773 goat¦354773 each day¦354775 as a¦354772 sin¦354772 offering¦354772.
\v 24 Then¦354777 the leader will perform a¦354777 food offering¦354777 of twenty-two litres \add of flour\add* with seven litres of \add olive\add* oil¦354783 for¦354779 each bull¦354779 and¦354780 each ram¦354781.
\p
\v 25 On¦354788 the fifteenth day¦354790 of¦354791 the seventh month¦354791 \add of the year\add*, at¦354792 the \add ‘temporary shelters’\add* celebration¦354792, the leader will¦354793 perform offerings on those¦354794 seven¦354795 days¦354796: Sin offerings¦354797, burnt offerings¦354798, food offerings¦354799, and¦354800 \add ≡offerings\add* of oil¦354800.\x + \xo 45:25: \xt Lev 23:33-36; Num 29:12-38.\x*
\c 46
\s1 The leader and the festivals
\rem /s1 Miscellaneous Regulations; The Prince and the Festivals
\p
\v 1 The master¦354806 Yahweh¦354807 says¦354805 this: The¦354808 eastern¦354812 gate¦354808 of¦354808 the¦354810 inner¦354810 courtyard¦354809 will¦354813 be¦354813 shut¦354814 for the¦354815 six¦354815 days¦354816 of¦354816 work¦354817, but¦354818 it'll¦354820 be¦354820 opened¦354820 on rest days and¦354821 on¦354821 the¦354821 day¦354821 of¦354821 the¦354822 new¦354822 moon¦354822.
\v 2 The¦354826 leader¦354826 will¦354825 enter the outer courtyard by the path¦354827 to that gate¦354829 and its outside¦354830 porch¦354828, and¦354831 he'll¦354831 stand¦354831 at¦354832 the¦354834 \add #doorposts¦354834\add* of¦354834 the¦354835 inner gate¦354835 while the¦354837 priests¦354837 perform his¦354840 burnt¦354840 offering and¦354841 peace¦354843 offering. Then¦354844 he'll¦354844 worship¦354844 \add ≈right there\add* at¦354845 the inner gate¦354848 and¦354849 \add ≈then leave¦354849 again\add*, but¦354850 the¦354850 gate¦354850 won't¦354851 be¦354853 shut¦354853 until¦354854 evening¦354856.
\v 3 The¦354859 people¦354859 will¦354858 also worship¦354858 in¦354867 front¦354867 of¦354862 Yahweh¦354868 at the¦354863 entrance¦354863 to that¦354864 gate¦354863 on¦354865 rest days and¦354866 new moons¦354866.
\v 4 The¦354870 burnt¦354870 offering¦354870 that¦354871 the¦354874 leader¦354874 offers to¦354875 Yahweh¦354875 on¦354876 the rest day¦354876 will¦354878 be¦354878 six¦354878 unblemished lambs¦354879 and an unblemished ram¦354881.
\v 5 The grain¦354884 offering¦354884 that he gives with the ram¦354886 is twenty-two litres \add of flour\add*, and¦354887 with the lambs¦354887 it should be¦354889 as much as he pleases, along with one litre of¦354889 \add olive\add* oil¦354891 for every twenty-two litres of flour.
\v 6 On¦354895 the¦354895 day¦354895 of¦354895 the¦354896 new¦354896 moon¦354896 he must offer a¦354897 bull¦354897, six¦354902 lambs¦354903, and¦354904 a¦354904 ram¦354904—\add ≈all without any defects\add*.
\v 7 He¦354912 must¦354912 \add also\add* provide¦354912 a¦354913 grain¦354913 offering¦354913 of twenty-two litres of flour with the bull¦354909, the same with the ram¦354911, and¦354914 as much as he wants with the lambs¦354914, along with a litre of \add olive\add* oil¦354918 with every twenty-two litres of flour.
\v 8 The¦354923 leader¦354923 enters by the¦354924 path¦354924 to the¦354926 gate¦354926 and¦354928 its¦354928 porch¦354925, and he¦354929 must¦354929 leave¦354929 by the same path.
\p
\v 9 But¦354931 when¦354931 the¦354934 land's¦354934 people¦354932 come¦354931 in¦354935 front¦354935 of¦354935 Yahweh¦354936 at¦354937 the¦354938 scheduled festivals, anyone¦354938 entering through the north gate to¦354943 worship¦354943 must¦354944 leave¦354944 through the south gate, and¦354949 anyone¦354949 entering through the south gate must¦354954 leave¦354954 through the north gate¦354957. No one is allowed to¦354958 turn¦354960 back to the¦354962 gate¦354962 through which¦354963 they entered, because¦354967 everyone must¦354969 keep going¦354969 straight ahead until they get out¦354969.
\v 10 The¦354972 leader¦354972 must¦354973 be¦354973 amongst¦354973 them¦354973 in their midst—when¦354974 they¦354974 go in, he¦354975 must¦354975 go in, and¦354976 when¦354976 they¦354976 leave¦354976, he must¦354977 leave¦354977.
\v 11 During the festivals¦354979 \add that you hold for me\add*, the¦354982 king must¦354981 present twenty-two litres of flour along with a bull¦354984 or a ram¦354986, and¦354987 as much flour as he wants to with the lambs¦354987, along with a litre of¦354988 \add olive\add* oil¦354990 with every twenty-two litres of flour.
\p
\v 12 When¦354995 the¦354998 leader¦354998 gives a¦354999 voluntary offering¦354999 to¦355005 Yahweh¦355005 (either a¦355000 burnt¦355000 offering¦355000 or¦355001 a peace offering¦355004), the¦355010 eastern¦355012 gate will¦355006 be opened¦355006 for him¦355007. He'll¦355013 offer¦355013 his¦355016 burnt¦355016 offering¦355019 or his¦355019 peace¦355019 offering just¦355020 like¦355020 he¦355021 does on¦355022 the rest day¦355022. Then¦355024 he¦355024 must¦355024 go¦355024 out¦355024, and after¦355029 he's¦355030 gone¦355030 out¦355030, the gate will be shut¦355025.
\s1 The daily sacrifice
\rem /s1 The Daily Offering
\p
\v 13 In addition¦355032, \add ≈every morning someone\add*\f + \fr 46:13 \ft Some of the earliest translations (the Septuagint and the Vulgate) have ‘he’ here, suggesting it must be the leader.\f* \add ≈must provide\add* a¦355032 one year¦355035 old lamb¦355032 with \add ≈no¦355036 defects¦355036\add* to be \add ≈completely burnt¦355038 up as an\add* offering¦355038 to¦355039 Yahweh¦355040.
\v 14 \add Also\add* \add ≈every morning\add* someone must¦355047 provide an offering¦355047 of¦355051 four litres of¦355054 flour¦355059 mixed with a¦355060 litre of¦355062 \add olive\add* oil¦355053, \add and\add* \add ≈that's a permanent¦355063 requirement\add*.
\v 15 They'll prepare the¦355070 lamb¦355070, the¦355073 grain¦355073 offering¦355073, and¦355074 the¦355076 oil¦355076, \add ≈every morning\add*—a¦355079 permanent \add daily\add* burnt¦355079 offering¦355079.
\s1 The leader and the country
\rem /s1 The Prince and the Land
\p
\v 16 The master¦355086 Yahweh¦355087 says¦355085 this: If¦355088 the¦355091 leader¦355091 gives¦355090 a¦355092 gift¦355092 to¦355093 any of¦355094 his sons¦355094, it becomes his¦355094 inheritance—that is¦355095, it'll¦355098 become his¦355095 sons'¦355097 property¦355099 \add because\add* it's¦355100 an¦355101 inheritance¦355101.
\v 17 \add ≈But¦355103\add* if he¦355105 gives¦355105 a¦355106 gift¦355106 from¦355107 his inheritance to¦355108 one¦355108 of¦355109 his¦355109 servants¦355109, then¦355110 it'll¦355110 belong¦355110 to that servant until¦355112 the¦355114 year¦355114 of¦355114 liberty¦355115, and¦355116 then it'll¦355116 return¦355116 to¦355117 the leader¦355117.\x + \xo 46:17: \xt Lev 25:10.\x* His inheritance¦355119 must definitely be for his¦355119 \add own\add* sons¦355120.
\v 18 The¦355127 leader¦355127 mustn't take¦355126 the¦355128 people's inheritance¦355128 away¦355126 from them, evicting them from¦355131 their¦355131 own¦355132 property¦355132—he¦355133 must¦355133 provide for his sons¦355136 from his¦355136 own property so¦355137 that my people won't¦355139 be¦355141 \add ≈forced off their own inherited¦355133 land\add*.”
\s1 The temple kitchens
\rem /s1 The Temple Kitchens
\p
\v 19 Next the man brought¦355146 me¦355146 through the entrance¦355147 at¦355149 the¦355152 gate¦355152 to¦355153 the¦355155 \add ≈sacred¦355156\add* rooms¦355155 for the¦355159 priests¦355159 which¦355160 were north-facing¦355161, and¦355162 what's more, there¦355164 was a¦355165 place¦355165 toward¦355168 the west¦355168.
\v 20 He¦355171 told¦355171 me¦355172, “That's the¦355174 place¦355174 where¦355175 the¦355179 priests¦355179 must boil¦355176 the¦355182 guilt¦355182 offering¦355182 and¦355183 the¦355185 sin¦355185 offering¦355185 and where¦355186 they¦355187 must¦355187 bake¦355187 the¦355190 grain¦355190 offering¦355190. They mustn't bring¦355192 the offerings into¦355193 the¦355196 outer¦355196 courtyard¦355195, because then the¦355200 people¦355200 would become sacred¦355197 \add (and not be able to leave)\add*.”
\p
\v 21 Then¦355202 he¦355202 brought¦355202 me¦355202 to¦355203 the¦355206 outer¦355206 courtyard and¦355207 led me¦355207 past the¦355210 four¦355210 corners¦355211 of¦355211 that courtyard¦355212, and¦355213 I saw¦355213 that in¦355215 every corner¦355215 of¦355215 the¦355216 courtyard there was¦355218 \add another\add* \add ≈enclosed area\add*,
\v 22 and each was¦355226 twenty-two metres long¦355227 and¦355228 sixteen metres wide¦355229.
\v 23 Around the¦355240 inside of¦355240 each of¦355240 those¦355240 enclosed areas was¦355242 a stone ledge, with cooking hearths \add all around\add* under¦355243 the¦355244 ledge.
\v 24 The man told¦355247 me¦355248, “Those¦355249 are¦355250 the¦355250 places where¦355252 the temple workers will¦355253 boil¦355253 the¦355261 people's¦355261 \add #sacrifices¦355260\add*.”
\c 47
\s1 The healing river
\rem /s1 The Stream Flowing from the Temple; The River From the Temple; The River of Healing; Water Flowing from the Temple
\p
\v 1 Then¦355263 the man took¦355263 me¦355263 back¦355263 to¦355264 the¦355266 temple entrance¦355266, and¦355268 there was water flowing out¦355271 from¦355272 under¦355272 the¦355273 \add ≈front¦355278 edge of¦355273 the¦355274 building\add* toward¦355275 the¦355279 east¦355280, and¦355281 the¦355281 water¦355281 was¦355282 flowing down¦355282 the¦355284 south side¦355284 of¦355284 the¦355285 temple¦355285, to the right of the altar¦355288.\x + \xo 47:1: \xt Zech 14:8; Yhn 7:38; Rev 22:1.\x*
\v 2 \add ≈So¦355290\add* he¦355290 took¦355290 me¦355290 out¦355290 through the¦355291 northern¦355294 gate¦355293 and¦355295 led me¦355295 around¦355295 \add the outside\add* to¦355298 the¦355300 eastern¦355304 gate¦355300, and¦355305 look¦355305, the \add #water¦355307\add* was¦355308 flowing from¦355309 this gate on \add &its\add* south¦355312 side¦355311.
\v 3 As the¦355316 man¦355316 \add ≈started walking\add* toward the east¦355317 holding his measuring line in¦355319 his¦355319 hand¦355319, he¦355320 measured¦355320 off five hundred metres and¦355323 led me¦355323 through¦355322 the water, which was ankle-deep¦355326.
\v 4 Then¦355328 he¦355328 measured¦355328 another five hundred metres and¦355330 led me¦355330 through¦355336 the water, which was \add now\add* knee-deep, and¦355334 then¦355336 after another five hundred metres it was \add ≈up to the waist¦355338\add*.
\v 5 \add Finally\add* he¦355340 measured¦355340 off another five hundred metres, but the¦355351 water¦355351 had¦355350 become deep enough to swim¦355353 in and too deep to cross¦355347—it was \add now\add* a¦355354 river¦355354 that¦355355 couldn't be¦355358 walked¦355358 through¦355358.
\v 6 The man \add ≈asked¦355360\add* me¦355361, “Son¦355363 of¦355363 humanity¦355365, \add ≈have you taken¦355367 note of all\add* this?”
\p Then¦355366 he¦355366 led¦355366 me¦355366 out and had me \add ≈return\add* along the riverbank.
\v 7 As I¦355371 walked back, wow¦355372, \add now\add* the riverbank had many¦355378 trees¦355377 on each side.
\v 8 The man told¦355383 me¦355384, “This water¦355385 \add ≈flows eastward and¦355392 down¦355392 into the Rift Valley\add*. \add *It'll\add* flow into the Dead Sea and¦355402 will¦355402 make it into fresh water¦355404.
\v 9 \add ≈As a result,\add* every¦355407 kind of¦355407 living¦355410 creature¦355409 that¦355412 swarms¦355414 will live¦355422 where that \add #water\add* flows. There'll be¦355423 lots of fish¦355424 when that \add #water\add* flows \add @into the Dead Sea\add* as it causes the¦355424 salty water to¦355429 become fresh water¦355430—everything¦355434 will¦355433 live¦355433 wherever¦355435 that river¦355439 goes.
\v 10 Then¦355441 the fishermen¦355445 from¦355446 Eyn¦355446-Gedi¦355447 will stand¦355442 \add there\add* by the water, and¦355448 there'll be a¦355452 place¦355452 to dry out the fishing nets¦355453 near Eyn-Eglayim¦355451. There'll be¦355454 many¦355461 \add #kinds¦355455\add* of¦355455 fish¦355457 in the Dead Sea—plentiful just like¦355458 the¦355458 fish¦355458 in the¦355459 Mediterranean Sea¦355459.
\v 11 \add However,\add* the Dead Sea's swamps¦355464 and¦355466 marshes¦355466 won't¦355467 be made fresh—they'll¦355468 be¦355468 for providing salt¦355469.
\v 12 Beside that river¦355474 on¦355476 both \add #banks¦355478\add*, all¦355483 kinds of¦355483 fruit \add #trees¦355485\add* will¦355490 grow¦355475. Their leaves¦355491 will never wilt, and¦355492 \add ≈they'll never fail to fruit¦355495\add*—\add in fact\add* \add *they'll\add* bear¦355497 fruit every¦355483 month¦355496 because¦355498 that \add #water¦355499\add* from¦355500 the¦355502 temple flows to them. Their fruit will¦355505 be¦355505 \add ≈good to eat\add*, and¦355509 their leaves¦355509 will¦355510 \add ≈help with\add* healing¦355510.\x + \xo 47:12: \xt Rev 22:2.\x*
\s1 Land boundaries
\rem /s1 Division of the land among the tribes; Boundaries for the Land; The New Boundaries of the Land; The Boundaries of the Land
\p
\v 13 The master¦355515 Yahweh¦355516 says¦355514 that this¦355517 will be¦355518 the¦355518 way that¦355519 you¦355520 divide the¦355523 land¦355523 up for Yisrael's¦355527 \add !(Israel¦355527)\add* twelve tribes¦355526: Yosef¦355528 \add !(Joseph¦355528)\add* will have \add ≈a double allocation\add*.
\v 14 You're¦355531 to equally divide¦355531 \add the land\add* that¦355535 I¦355536 \add ≈took action\add* to¦355540 give¦355540 to¦355541 your¦355541 ancestors¦355541—\add from now on\add* that land¦355543 will¦355542 come to¦355545 you¦355545 \add Israelis\add* as an¦355546 inheritance¦355546.
\p
\v 15 \add #These will be¦355549 the land¦355550 boundaries¦355549\add*: On the¦355551 north¦355552 side¦355551, it'll extend from¦355553 the¦355555 Mediterranean Sea¦355555 east along the road towards Hetlon¦355558, and then on to¦355560 Tsedad¦355560,
\v 16 to Berotah¦355564, and then on to Sivrayim¦355565, which¦355566 is¦355567 on the¦355569 border¦355569 between Damesek¦355570 \add !(Damascus¦355570)\add* and¦355571 Hamat. The¦355572 boundary will run as far as Hatsar¦355574-Hatikon¦355575, which¦355576 is¦355577 on the¦355579 border¦355579 of¦355579 the Havran¦355580 \add region\add*.
\v 17 \add ≈So¦355582\add* the¦355583 boundary¦355583 will extend from¦355584 the¦355586 Mediterranean Sea¦355586 to Hatsar¦355587-Eynan¦355588 on the¦355589 border¦355589 between Hamat to¦355593 the¦355594 north and¦355596 Damesek¦355590 \add to the south\add*. \add ≈That'll be the northern¦355598 boundary\add*.
\p
\v 18 On the¦355600 eastern side¦355600, the¦355601 boundary will extend between¦355602 Havran¦355603 and¦355604 Damesek¦355606, south along the¦355612 Yarden¦355612 \add !(Jordan¦355612)\add* \add River\add* between¦355607 the¦355608 Gilead¦355608 \add region\add* and¦355609 Yisrael's¦355611 territory¦355610, along the Dead Sea¦355616 as far as Tamar.\f + \fr 47:18 \ft Tamar: the Hebrew has ‘the Eastern Sea’.\f* \add ≈That'll be the¦355617 eastern¦355617 boundary\add*.
\p
\v 19 On the¦355623 southern side¦355623, the¦355624 boundary will¦355626 extend from¦355626 Tamar¦355626 to¦355627 the¦355629 springs near Merivah-Kadesh¦355631. Then it'll extend \add west\add* along the¦355632 brook of Mitsrayim \add !(Egypt)\add* to¦355633 the¦355635 Mediterranean Sea¦355635. \add ≈That'll be the southern boundary\add*.
\p
\v 20 On the¦355643 western side¦355643, the¦355645 boundary¦355648 will be the¦355646 Mediterranean Sea¦355646, \add north\add* to¦355649 a point near Levo¦355652-Hamat.
\p
\v 21 This is how you'll¦355659 divide¦355659 this land¦355662 for¦355664 yourselves among Yisrael's¦355666 tribes¦355665:
\v 22 You¦355669 must¦355669 assign the land as a permanent possession for¦355672 yourselves and¦355673 also¦355673 for¦355673 any foreigners who¦355674 are living¦355674 and raising their children¦355679 among¦355680 you. You must¦355681 consider them to be like¦355683 native¦355683 born Israelis¦355685, and they¦355687 must¦355687 be assigned land among Yisrael's¦355691 tribes¦355690.
\v 23 Wherever foreigners are¦355693 living, you¦355701 must¦355701 give¦355701 them some land \add ≈to belong to them permanently\add*. That's the¦355703 master¦355704 Yahweh's¦355705 declaration¦355703.”
\c 48
\s1 Land allocated to Yisrael's tribes
\rem /s1 Division of the Land; The Division of the Land; The Division of the Land among the Tribes; The Tribal Portions
\p
\v 1 Here's a list of¦355709 Yisrael's \add !(Israel)\add* tribes¦355710 and the¦355711 territory that each tribe is to¦355713 receive. The¦355715 northern boundary of¦355715 Yisrael will start at the¦355716 Mediterranean Sea and go \add east\add* to Hetlon¦355718 \add city\add*, then to Levo¦355720-Hamat, and farther on to Hatsar¦355723-Eynan¦355724, which is \add south\add* of Damesek¦355726 \add !(Damascus¦355726)\add*, and it'll continue to Hamat. Each tribe will receive land that will extend from the eastern¦355737 boundary of Yisrael west to the¦355738 Mediterranean Sea¦355738.
\rem /s1 The Special Section in the Center of the Land
\p The tribe of Dan¦355739 will receive land alongside Yisrael's northern border¦355725.
\v 2 \add ≈South of their area\add* will be the territory for \add the tribe of\add* Asher¦355753.
\v 3 \add ≈South of¦355758\add* Asher's¦355759 land will be territory for \add the tribe of\add* Naftali¦355767 \add !(Naphtali¦355767)\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈South of¦355772\add* Naftali's¦355773 land will be territory for \add the tribe of\add* Menasheh¦355781 \add !(Manasseh¦355781)\add*.
\v 5 \add ≈South of¦355786\add* Menasheh's¦355787 land will be territory for \add the tribe of\add* Efrayim¦355795 \add !(Ephraim¦355795)\add*.
\v 6 \add ≈South of¦355800\add* Efrayim's¦355801 land will be territory for \add the tribe of\add* Re'uven¦355809.
\v 7 \add ≈South of¦355814\add* Re'uven's¦355815 land will be territory for \add the tribe of\add* Yehudah¦355823 \add !(Judah¦355823)\add*.
\s1 The chosen place
\p
\v 8 \add ≈South of¦355827\add* Yehudah's¦355828 \add !(Judah¦355828)\add* land will¦355836 be¦355836 an area that¦355838 the¦355837 entire nation will give to me—you¦355840 \add Israelis\add* will¦355840 set it apart for special use. The temple will¦355841 be¦355841 in¦355857 the¦355857 middle¦355857 of¦355857 the area that's thirteen kilometres wide¦355844 and¦355855 as long¦355845 as¦355846 the other land allocations.
\p
\v 9 The¦355859 special area that¦355860 you¦355861 \add Israelis\add* will¦355861 give¦355861 to¦355862 Yahweh¦355862 will¦355864 be¦355864 thirteen kilometres long¦355863 and five¦355864 kilometres wide¦355867.
\v 10 This special area will be allocated to the¦355873 priests¦355876: you'll assign them an area of¦355873 land measuring thirteen kilometres on the¦355875 northern¦355877 and¦355879 southern¦355889 sides, and¦355881 five kilometres on¦355881 the¦355882 western¦355881 and¦355885 eastern¦355885 sides. Yahweh's¦355897 temple will be in¦355898 the¦355898 middle¦355898 of¦355898 that special area.
\v 11 The temple area will be for the priests¦355900, Tsadok's¦355903 descendants¦355902, who¦355904 are dedicated¦355901 to serving me. (They're¦355905 the ones who¦355907 served me faithfully and didn't¦355908 turn away \add from me\add* like¦355914 the¦355916 Levites¦355916 \add ≈and the others\add* did.)
\v 12 The \add ≈priests' allocation\add* will¦355919 be a¦355921 portion of¦355922 that very sacred land¦355923, extending to¦355926 the¦355929 Levites'¦355929 border¦355928.
\v 13 The¦355931 land assigned to¦355932 the Levites¦355931 will¦355935 be¦355935 the same size as the land that the¦355934 priests¦355934 will¦355940 receive. (So together, these two portions of land will¦355945 be¦355945 thirteen kilometres long and about ten¦355949 kilometres wide.)
\v 14 They¦355954 mustn't sell¦355954 or barter it¦355955—none of this best¦355961 part of¦355961 Yisrael's \add !(Israel)\add* land¦355962 must ever be \add ≈used for any other purpose\add*, because¦355963 it's¦355965 all sacred¦355965 to¦355966 Yahweh¦355966.
\p
\v 15 Another strip of land¦355983 thirteen kilometres long and¦355976 almost three kilometres wide¦355971 will¦355978 be¦355978 allotted for other people living¦355982 in the special area to use. They may build homes and have pasturelands there, and¦355984 there'll be¦355984 a city¦355985 in¦355986 the¦355986 middle¦355986 of¦355986 that¦355970 area¦355978.
\v 16 The city \add ≈will be square, over two kilometres long on each side\add*,
\v 17 with an open¦356021 area around it¦356020, within the special area, that will be¦356020 about 130m wide in each direction.
\v 18 Outside the city, there'll be two strips that'll extend five kilometres on each of the east¦356044 and¦356045 west¦356047 sides, and¦356048 the produce¦356053 from \add @those strips\add* will be food¦356055 for¦356056 the people working in the¦356057 city¦356057.
\v 19 \add ≈Those who¦356059 come from¦356062 the¦356063 various tribes¦356063 to work in the city¦356060 may also¦356059 work in this farmland\add*.
\v 20 That entire¦356066 special area, including the¦356068 land given for Yahweh's use and the¦356084 city¦356084, will be a square that's thirteen kilometres long on each side.
\p
\v 21 The tracts of land to¦356097 the east and¦356101 to¦356105 the¦356107 west¦356109 of¦356112 Yahweh's area and¦356114 the¦356096 city¦356096 will¦356122 belong to¦356122 the¦356122 leader¦356122. One area will¦356123 extend thirteen kilometres east to Yisrael's eastern boundary, and¦356126 the¦356126 other will extend thirteen kilometres west \add to the Mediterranean Sea\add*. Yahweh's area, which¦356086 contains the¦356127 temple¦356127, will be in¦356128 the¦356128 middle¦356128.
\rem /s1 Land for the Other Tribes
\v 22 The area that belongs to the leader (that surrounds the Levites'¦356132 property and¦356133 the¦356134 city¦356134 in¦356135 the¦356135 middle¦356135 of¦356136 it¦356138) will¦356138 be between¦356139 the¦356141 tribe of¦356141 Yehudah¦356142 \add !(Judah¦356142)\add* \add to the north\add* and¦356143 the¦356144 tribe of¦356144 Benyamin¦356145 \add to the south\add*.
\p
\v 23 South of Yahweh's area, each of the¦356150 other tribes¦356150 will receive one¦356159 section of land that extends from¦356151 the¦356151 eastern¦356152 \add boundary of Yisrael\add* west¦356157 \add all the way to the Mediterranean Sea.\add*
\p Just south of Yahweh's area will be territory for the¦356155 tribe of¦356155 Benyamin¦356158.
\v 24 \add ≈South of¦356163\add* Benyamin's¦356164 land will be the land for \add the tribe of\add* Shim'on¦356172 \add !(Simeon¦356172)\add*.
\v 25 \add ≈South of¦356177\add* Shim'on's¦356178 land will be the land for \add the tribe of\add* Yisakar¦356186 \add !(Issachar¦356186)\add*.
\v 26 \add ≈South of¦356191\add* Yisakar's¦356192 land will be the land for \add the tribe of\add* Zevulun¦356200.
\v 27 \add ≈South of¦356205\add* Zevulun's¦356206 land will be the land for \add the tribe of\add* Gad¦356214.
\v 28 Gad's¦356219 southern boundary¦356226 will extend from¦356227 Tamar¦356227 \add south\add* to the¦356228 waters¦356228 of¦356228 Merivah¦356229-Kadesh¦356230, and farther \add west\add* to the¦356231 brook \add of Mitsrayim\add* \add !(Egypt)\add*, and then on¦356232 to the¦356234 Mediterranean Sea¦356234.
\p
\v 29 That¦356237 \add describes\add* the¦356238 territory¦356238 you'll¦356241 allocate to¦356243 Yisrael's¦356244 tribes¦356243 to become their¦356246 permanent inheritance¦356242. That's the¦356247 master¦356248 Yahweh's¦356249 declaration¦356247.
\s1 The city gateways
\rem /s1 The Gates of the City; The Gates of Jerusalem
\p
\v 30 These¦356252 will¦356253 be¦356253 the¦356253 exits¦356253 from the¦356254 city¦356254: On the¦356255 northern¦356256 side¦356255 (which will¦356257 be¦356257 just over two kilometres long),
\v 31 there'll be¦356265 three¦356271 gates named¦356267 after the¦356268 tribes¦356268 of¦356268 Re'uven¦356274, Yehudah¦356277 \add !(Judah¦356277)\add*, and Levi¦356280.
\v 32 On the¦356285 eastern¦356286 side¦356285 (the same length) there'll be¦356287 three¦356292 gates¦356293 named after \add the tribes of\add* Yosef¦356294 \add !(Joseph¦356294)\add*, Benyamin¦356297, and Dan¦356300.
\v 33 On the¦356303 southern¦356305 side¦356303 (the same length) there'll be¦356306 three¦356312 gates¦356313 named after \add the tribes of\add* Shim'on¦356314 \add !(Simeon¦356314)\add*, Yisakar¦356317 \add !(Issachar¦356317)\add*, and Zevulun¦356320.
\v 34 On the¦356323 western¦356325 side¦356323 (the same length) there'll be¦356326 three¦356331 gates¦356332 named after \add the tribes of\add* Gad¦356333, Asher¦356336, and Naftali¦356339 \add !(Naphtali¦356339)\add*.\x + \xo 48:30-34: \xt Rev 21:12-13.\x*
\v 35 The¦356342 \add ≈distance around the city\add* will be nine kilometres.
\p From¦356349 that time¦356349 \add onwards\add*, the city's name¦356346 will¦356350 be¦356350 ‘Yahweh¦356350 is¦356352 there’.
